WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.050
Here's a benchmark task.

2
00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:05.195
How many orders have a null customer ID?

3
00:00:05.315 --> 00:00:07.215
The answer is 7.

4
00:00:07.335 --> 00:00:10.915
A scripted agent answers 17, and the naive

5
00:00:10.915 --> 00:00:14.835
grader passes it, because 7 appears inside
17.

6
00:00:14.955 --> 00:00:20.380
Public reports, like OSWorld issue #518,
describe exactly this.

7
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.850
When the grader is wrong, the score
measures the grader, not the agent.

8
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.175
This prototype grades the grader, without
running a model.

9
00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:34.345
Twelve synthetic data-engineering tasks
each get eight scripted probe outputs.

10
00:00:34.465 --> 00:00:39.890
Gold, paraphrase, no-op, substring trap, a
fake that only looks done, and more.

11
00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:43.135
All 96 cases have a known label.

12
00:00:43.255 --> 00:00:46.480
A false positive passes a case that should
fail.

13
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.675
A false negative fails one that should
pass.

14
00:00:49.795 --> 00:00:52.620
A flaky verdict changes between runs.

15
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.120
It's a straight pipeline.

16
00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.365
tasks.py generates 12 tasks from one fixed
seed.

17
00:00:59.485 --> 00:01:03.022
probes.py adds eight labeled outputs per
task,

18
00:01:03.022 --> 00:01:06.635
and generate.py writes them to the data
folder.

19
00:01:06.755 --> 00:01:12.855
runner.py grades every case five times per
grader, then writes results and a report.

20
00:01:12.975 --> 00:01:17.950
serve.py, a FastAPI app, serves them to
one static page.

21
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.250
Let's follow that 17 through the code.

22
00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:26.395
run_audit loads each labeled case, and
calls _grade_k for every grader.

23
00:01:26.515 --> 00:01:32.265
_grade_k runs it five times, with seeds
zero to four, and takes the majority vote.

24
00:01:32.385 --> 00:01:36.960
The naive grader asks one question: is the
gold string in the output?

25
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.280
It is, so it scores one.

26
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:44.150
The hardened grader normalizes both
strings and needs an exact match.

27
00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.820
That fails, so it asks the judge.

28
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:50.074
That's a deterministic mock by default,

29
00:01:50.074 --> 00:01:53.690
and it returns numeric mismatch, 17 versus
7.

30
00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:55.410
Score zero.

31
00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:00.455
Finally, a pass on a should-fail case is
recorded as a false positive.

32
00:02:01.055 --> 00:02:03.480
Tables show the same contrast.

33
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.557
The naive grader checks the header and the
row

34
00:02:06.557 --> 00:02:09.900
count, so a table with one wrong total
still passes.

35
00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:14.220
The hardened grader normalizes each cell
by its declared type.

36
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:21.090
So 1.50 equals 1.5, and month-first dates
are rejected as ambiguous.

37
00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:24.960
Then it checks the row count, unique keys,
and every row.

38
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.360
Why five runs?

39
00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.855
Some graders depend on time.

40
00:02:29.975 --> 00:02:32.050
Task S1 plants one.

41
00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.602
Its naive grader fails a run when

42
00:02:34.602 --> 00:02:37.770
"finished at" is more than 300 seconds
old.

43
00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:42.740
The clock is simulated from the seed, up
to ten minutes after a fixed time.

44
00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:46.585
So the correct answer passes three runs
and fails two.

45
00:02:46.705 --> 00:02:49.930
The majority says pass, but it's flagged
flaky.

46
00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.325
It's the only grader flagged.

47
00:02:52.925 --> 00:02:58.025
To run it, install the requirements, then
python -m audit.serve.

48
00:02:58.145 --> 00:03:00.961
That regenerates the data, runs the audit,

49
00:03:00.961 --> 00:03:03.845
and serves the page on port eight
thousand.

50
00:03:03.965 --> 00:03:06.265
No API key is needed.

51
00:03:06.385 --> 00:03:11.028
On these 96 synthetic cases, the naive
graders pass

52
00:03:11.028 --> 00:03:16.035
20 of 49 wrong outputs, and fail 14 of 47
correct ones.

53
00:03:16.155 --> 00:03:18.880
The hardened graders make zero errors.

54
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.450
152 tests pass, including a mutation test.

55
00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:25.850
Now, the caveats.

56
00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:28.799
The hardened graders score zero by
construction,

57
00:03:28.799 --> 00:03:31.570
because they were written knowing these
probes.

58
00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.579
One gotcha: the naive state grader checks

59
00:03:34.579 --> 00:03:37.890
status by substring, so "not succeeded"
passes.

60
00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:42.960
The flakiness is simulated, and the real
LLM judge was never run.

61
00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:48.580
Labels are assumed known, and the tasks
are synthetic files, not browser state.

62
00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:53.275
Next: adversarial probes from an LLM,
reviewed by a human.

63
00:03:53.395 --> 00:03:58.545
To recap: known labels, two graders, five
seeded runs, one command.
